QSA: bf16 block-score buffer and bf16 tensor-core dots in the prefill scorer

The QSA full-scan (prefill) streams one fp32 score per complete block through
_scores_tc's writes and _select/_select_tiles' reads: 512KB per layer-row at
512k, the dominant DRAM term of the ~56%-of-prefill attn stage (measured
2026-10-03, TENSORFOLD_DIAG_TIMING: attn 1400ms of ~2500ms per 4096-row pass).

Scores are only ever used to order blocks (the fp32 bitcast trick makes the
float order an integer order, ties to lower block id). bf16 keeps that order
up to collisions of two distinct scores that round to the same bf16 value,
which can only reorder near-cutoff candidates (the tie rule then applies, as
for true ties). This patch:
  - the score buffer is bf16 (half the write and select-read traffic);
  - _scores_tc dots run on bf16 tensor cores (the fp32 upconverts are dropped;
    fp32 accumulation is kept) -- the v1 idea behind 0006's tl.dot scorer;
  - the order-preserving key is the 16-bit bf16 bitcast (sign-bit flip,
    scores are relu sums so non-negative), the _select bisection runs 17
    steps over [0, 2^16), and _select_tiles' radix select uses two byte
    passes instead of four.

Every consumer of the score buffer (_select, _select_tiles, _reuse_select,
_block_keys) reads the same bf16 buffer, so decode/reuse rows are consistent
with prefill rows. Expected: prefill score/select DRAM halved; selection
differs from the fp32 build only where bf16 rounding collides two distinct
scores -- the 512k needles/thinking/vision gates decide.

